 "D354": {"key": "exposure", "value": {"cold": 10, "span": 20, "ration": 0.4, "sick": 1, "nerve": 0.3}, "name": "exposure to the weather (Albert, Nov 8 18:39-18:41): a side's share, 0 to 1, is its cold (the air under cold °C, full span °C lower) × its cold cut (Wool cloaks) plus its wet (the ground's wetness) × its wet cut (Oiled cloaks), the whole × its chill cut (Chimneys); everyone eats (1 + ration × exposure) × their meals (the US Army's cold-weather ration, 4,500 against 3,250 kcal a day, N275), the town's cases from outside are × (1 + sick × exposure) (cold and wet raise respiratory infection, N276), and a soldier's nerve settles no higher than full × (1 − nerve × exposure) (cold and wet sap morale, N277); research compounds and never takes it to nothing (QCA)", "cite": ["N275", "N276", "N277"], "law": ["QCA"]},
