  "D360": {"key": "woods", "value": {"enter": {"person": 1, "horse": 1}, "eta": 1.5, "walk": 1.7, "stems": 0.05, "trunk": 0.3, "edge": 4, "ring": 3, "thick": 2, "dash": [3, 2.5], "pulse": 1.6, "low": 0.35, "cover": 6, "side": 2, "box": [120, 220, 20]}, "name": "(box: how far a woods chunk's picture reaches past its ground to each side, up and down, in view units, so the tallest and widest tree (216 up, 117 across, measured Nov 10) is never cut at a chunk's edge; cover, side: every untouched tree is drawn once into the woods' cached picture; a tree is drawn again, live, over a person only when it stands in front of him, looked for cover tiles ahead of him and side tiles to each side (Albert, Nov 10 21:08-21:10: still things are not redrawn, only the trees being cut and the things a person stands behind); Albert, Nov 10 20:03-20:06: no silhouettes: a person of yours hidden under the canopy or behind a tree or building shows only a ring, dashed (hidden, as a hidden edge is drawn dashed), thick times the foot ring's line, in his side's colour; an idle villager's ring glows and dims over pulse s, down to low, since he is the one asking to be found) (ring: your own people under the canopy drawn as a ring ring view units wide over the trees: Albert, Nov 9, seeing who is inside) the woods walkable at a cost (Albert, Nov 9 18:33, 19:59: the ice's crossing modes, real math for walking and fighting): who may enter, by what carries them (a person, a rider; engines, wagons and beasts never); eta the Pandolf terrain factor for heavy brush (Soule and Goldman 1972: 1.5), so the pace falls by the same physics as mud and snow; walk a horse's walk in m/s (about 6 km/h: low boughs keep a rider to it); stems a square metre and trunk the trunk's width in m (about 500 a hectare, 0.3 m), so an arrow or shot through L metres of wood is stopped with chance 1 - e^(-stems x trunk x L); edge the crossing's stick-to-edges weight a tile of woods by how deep in it is", "cite": ["N292", "N294", "N287"], "law": []},
 "D361": {"key": "fogwar", "value": {"dark": 0.88, "seen": 0.5, "cell": 4, "rays": 2, "rim": 16, "wall": 0, "see": 0.5}, "name": "(in or under woods an eye looks between the trunks: a ray goes on while the chance a line is still clear, e^(-stems x trunk x metres of wood, D360), stays above see, about 45 m; from above the canopy the wood is a wall (Albert, Nov 9 20:17: villagers in trees can\u2019t really see); sight in cells of cell × cell tiles, 5 m; each eye sends rays × π × reach rays, so neighbouring rays meet at the rim; a ray marks each cell whose ground, a body high, rises above the steepest top before it, and stops at a wall: ground steeper than the tops by wall; an eye whose rim, rim points round it, is all seen by others casts nothing (Albert, Nov 9 20:12-20:13: you see until the first thing that blocks; only the eyes facing the fog see)) the fog of war's veil over the map (Albert, Nov 9 item 6; StarCraft's three states, N290): ground never seen under dark, ground seen before under seen, ground in sight clear; the veil drawn a pixel a tile and smoothed, so its edge is soft and units fade in and out at the edge of sight", "cite": ["N290", "N287"], "law": []},
 "D362": {"key": "fogLayer", "value": {"depth": 100, "fov": 60, "rad": 0.0174533, "km": 1000, "step": 0.05}, "name": "fog seen from the camera (Albert, Nov 9 item 4): radiation fog a layer depth m deep (Roach: tens to a couple of hundred m); the camera's height from the zoom: the ground's width on the screen, half of it over tan(fov / 2), a phone camera's field of view in degrees; the light's path through the fog is the layer, or the camera's height when the camera is inside it, so zoomed out the fog is white and zoomed in you see through; km: metres a kilometre, as beta is per km; the layer's top lies depth over the lowest ground, so fog lies deep in the hollows and thin or gone on the hills (cold air drains downhill: valley fog, Roach); each 5 m cell gets its own veil; the sheet is painted again when beta or the camera's height moves by step", "cite": ["N291", "N287"], "law": []},
